{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# dao.stream.host-boundary: dao.stream wherever the boundary can be an IO action\n     2\t\n     3\tThe governing test is the owner's: **dao.stream is used wherever a host\n     4\tboundary can be an IO action; a pure, total, synchronous function on the same\n     5\thost may be a direct call.** This document's commissionable scope is that\n     6\trule (§2) and the pure-primitive slices H0-H4 (§8): freeze and allowlist; the\n     7\t`dao.host.*` integer, float, number, bytes and text profile; migration of\n     8\tevery consumer; hash and signature entries; the portable Jing CBOR writer.\n     9\tEntropy and scheduling, the durable-storage seam, the parser stage and the\n    10\tstream conformance suite are **deferred** to a separate design (§9) and are\n    11\tnot commissionable here.\n    12\t\n    13\tStatus: design only; nothing commissioned. **Revision r5**, 2026-10-20, by\n    14\tthe Architect (claude-fable-5-1). **Review status: SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\n    15\tby a non-Claude architect (gpt-6-astra, 2026-10-20,\n    16\t`collab/1792508286000-architect-host-boundary-review-r4.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`)\n    17\tfor the narrowed scope only, §2 plus H0-H4; H5-H8 (§9) are not signed off\n    18\tand remain deferred.** The four mechanical corrections M1-M4 that sign-off\n    19\trequired were applied in r5 (rationale\n    20\t`collab/1792508512000-architect-host-boundary-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`)\n    21\tand should be confirmed by a non-Claude reviewer before H0 is dispatched.\n    22\tr7 (2026-10-21): reconciled with what slice H0 measured (F1-F4: the 16\n    23\tunlisted host arms categorised, the closure-based effect counts, the §3.1\n    24\trows, the print and M-entry facts); rationale\n    25\t`collab/1792522578000-architect-host-boundary-r7.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n    26\t**a non-Claude reviewer must confirm this r7 before H1 is dispatched.**\n    27\tr6: D1-D3 applied (a non-Claude reviewer's confirmation check,\n    28\t`collab/1792517656000-reviewer-host-boundary-r5.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`,\n    29\tfound M1-M4 applied and three low defects; rationale\n    30\t`collab/1792518203000-architect-host-boundary-r6.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\n    31\tLineage: r1 rationale\n    32\t`collab/1792495909000-architect-host-boundary.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n    33\tr1 review NOT SIGNED OFF\n    34\t(`collab/1792498045000-architect-host-boundary-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n    35\tA1-A8); r2 rationale `collab/1792502807000-...-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n    36\tr2 review NOT SIGNED OFF\n    37\t(`collab/1792503557000-architect-host-boundary-review-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n    38\tR1-R6); r3 rationale and disposition\n    39\t`collab/1792506992000-architect-host-boundary-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n    40\tr3 scoped review NOT SIGNED OFF\n    41\t(`collab/1792507576000-architect-host-boundary-review-r3.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n    42\tN1-N6); **r4** answers N1-N6 within the unchanged scope (rationale\n    43\t`collab/1792507856000-architect-host-boundary-r4.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\n    44\tOwner decision for r3 (verbatim): \"yes, proceed with the split\". No code was\n    45\tchanged; every claim about the tree is labelled. **Because this document was\n    46\twritten by a Claude model, a non-Claude architect must sign off this final\n    47\ttext (§2 plus H0-H4) before anything is commissioned from it** (§14).\n    48\t\n    49\tOwner direction, verbatim. Original: \"maybe this is an opportunity to stop\n    50\tand refactor so that the dao.stream is the boundary that crosses over host\n    51\tspecific dependency. once that is done, the only thing needs porting is\n    52\tdao.stream implementations\". Refined: \"maybe my dao.stream ABI framing has to\n    53\tbe refined.  when a host-boundary is cross should it be used. when its on the\n    54\tsame host, then direct function call is fine. In other words, when the host\n    55\tboundary can be an IO monad then dao.stream is used. If it cannot be a monad\n    56\tthan dao.stream doesnt have to be used\". Asked which formulation governs:\n    57\t\"yes, test 2 governs\".\n    58\t\n    59\tAuthorities:\n    60\t- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md) `:43-48`, `:70`.\n    61\t- [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §3 custody; §4 machine-internal\n    62\t  work executes inside the instance; §5 the floor and internal linkage; §6\n    63\t  effects out, events in; §7 adapter discipline (`:143-148`). §12 is an\n    64\t  owner proposal to amend it; nothing here edits it.\n    65\t- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the contract; cursors are non-destructive\n    66\t  (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:159-173`, V via the r2 review).\n    67\t- [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §1 `:29-52`: seven imports.\n    68\t- [dao.stream.codec.md](./dao.stream.codec.md): the codec track; untouched\n    69\t  here (§8.3).\n    70\t- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5; [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md);\n    71\t  [dao.jing.cbor.md](./dao.jing.cbor.md).\n    72\t\n    73\tLabels: V = verified by reading this tree on 2026-10-20 (line cites); R =\n    74\tread, computed by script, or inferred, not run under a compiler; P =\n    75\tproposed. Nothing was executed on a lane.\n    76\t\n    77\tStandalone: a future host lane is an example consumer, never a dependency.\n    78\t\n    79\t---\n    80\t\n    81\t## 1. Problem\n    82\t\n    83\tV: the portable core (`src/cljc`, 183 files) reaches hosts two ways and the\n    84\tdoctrine sanctions only one. The sanctioned way is the transports' pattern:\n    85\ta portable core plus a per-host seam value the composition hands in\n    86\t(`yin.repl.host/websocket`, `host.cljc:20-37`; `datagram.jvm/bind!`,\n    87\t`jvm.clj:22-34`). The other way is host contact inside portable files: reader\n    88\tarms reaching a host library, and resolved calls to host-backed core\n    89\tfunctions that compile everywhere and still touch the world.\n    90\t\n    91\tThe in-scope files (V, `rg -c '#\\?'`; what the arms reach):\n    92\t\n    93\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    94\t| File                              | `#?`  | Host contact (V)                                                                                        |\n    95\t+===================================+=======+=========================================================================================================+\n    96\t| `dao/jing/cbor.cljc` (1562 lines) | 52    | Big-integer helpers (`:106-254`); `Float64`/`Rational`/`Decimal` carriers (`:342-460`); JVM             |\n    97\t|                                   |       | `BigDecimal`/`Ratio` construction and access (`:483-632`); float bits (`:525-541`); writer selection    |\n    98\t|                                   |       | (`:58-60`: Boring on JVM and JS, the Dart `cbor` package).                                              |\n    99\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   100\t| `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc` (299)  | 30    | 25 public functions (`:41-298`): carrier recognition, promotion, 13 big operations.                     |\n   101\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   102\t| `dao/jing.cljc` (629)             | 12    | BLAKE3 and SHA-256 libraries (`:32-40, :59-135`); an opaque sentinel (`:43-51`).                        |\n   103\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   104\t| `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (274)   | 16    | Ed25519 libraries; `jvm-pair` replays a given seed through a `SecureRandom` proxy (`:75-87`, V:         |\n   105\t|                                   |       | deterministic, not fresh entropy); `generate` draws fresh entropy (`:114-124`, deferred, §9).           |\n   106\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   107\t| `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`       | 14    | The two wire writers (`:cljd nil`-gated Boring wrapper; 107-line Dart twin).                            |\n   108\t| (132), `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cl |       |                                                                                                         |\n   109\t| jd.cljd`                          |       |                                                                                                         |\n   110\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   111\t| `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc` (16)       | 16    | Bytes predicate (`:167`), char access (`:241`), NFC (`:310-321`, Unicode 16.0 owner-settled), float     |\n   112\t|                                   |       | bits (`:855-885`), carrier tests (`:1255-1257`).                                                        |\n   113\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   114\t| `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc` (13)  | 13    | Float64 little-endian hex (`:232-242`); JVM big-integer predicate (`:315-317`).                         |\n   115\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   116\t| `yin/vm/data.cljc` (7),           | 7+9   | Code units and `units->string` (`:119-173`); `integral` (`:77-92`); `Closure`/`Continuation` equality,  |\n   117\t| `yin/vm/values.cljc` (9)          |       | hash and print arms (`:39-119`).                                                                        |\n   118\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   119\t| `dao/stream/chunks.cljc` (5),     | 5+4+4 | `Uint8List` length, alloc, slice, copy (`chunks.cljc:8-32`); base64 libraries; host-bytes predicate     |\n   120\t| `base64.cljc`, `codec.cljc`       |       | (`codec.cljc:45`, codec-track file, §8.3).                                                              |\n   121\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   122\t| `dao/jing/dht.cljc` (10),         | 10+14 | Bytes helpers (`dht.cljc:100-121`); bytes length (`linker.cljc:1232`); `host-bytes`/`utf8`              |\n   123\t| `yin/vm/linker.cljc`, `datagram`  |       | (`sign.cljc:55-66`); byte length (`datagram.cljc:63-66`).                                               |\n   124\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   125\t| `dao/data/arrays.cljc` (21)       | 21    | Host arrays, `^long` hints, `parallelSort` (`:145`).                                                    |\n   126\t+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   127\t\n   128\tOut of scope here and listed in §9: `dao/jing/file.cljc`, `dao/space/store/\n   129\tfs.cljc`, `dao/space/store.cljc`, `dao/stream/journal/file.cljc`,\n   130\t`dao/data/btree/storage.cljc` (storage); `dao/jing/content/async.cljc`\n   131\t(outside the closure, H0; recorded for the deferred design),\n   132\t`dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:80` and `src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj`\n   133\t(scheduling, blocking driver); every entropy site; `yang/python/antlr/\n   134\tparser.cljc` and the `yang.antlr.cst` hosts (parser stage).\n   135\t\n   136\tV, the duplication: `yin.vm.integer.host` says it \"does not reach into\n   137\t`dao.jing.cbor`'s private big-integer helpers\" (`host.cljc:23-25`); `cbor.cljc`\n   138\tkeeps its own (`:112-216`). Two shims, one contract.\n   139\t\n   140\t## 2. The governing rule\n   141\t\n   142\t### 2.1 The boundary, defined first (review R1)\n   143\t\n   144\t**The machine** is the running instance and the state it owns: its values,\n   145\tand the mutable runtime state its own code created and no other party can\n   146\tobserve or change: atoms and volatiles, host arrays and byte arrays it\n   147\tallocated, a B-tree's weak or soft references, the collector that clears\n   148\tthem, thread-local locks between threads the driver created. **The world** is\n   149\teverything else: files, sockets, other processes, the operating system's\n   150\tclock, entropy sources, environment, the terminal, and any other instance.\n   151\t\n   152\tA host contact is **external** when it observes or changes the world. It is\n   153\t**internal** when it is a function of the machine's values and owned runtime\n   154\tstate only.\n   155\t\n   156\t### 2.2 Statement\n   157\t\n   158\t**An external contact uses `dao.stream`: it can be an IO action. An internal\n   159\tcontact that is a pure, total, synchronous function of its arguments, or a\n   160\tdeclared mutation of an owned argument (§2.4), may be a direct call and is\n   161\tthen an entry of the primitive profile (§4.3).**\n   162\t\n   163\tThis is the owner's test 2. Same-host-ness is not the criterion: a same-host\n   164\tfile read is external and is a stream; a hash from a library linked into the\n   165\tinstance is internal and is a direct call.\n   166\t\n   167\t### 2.3 \"Can be an IO action\", operationally\n   168\t\n   169\tA contact can be an IO action when any of these holds **with respect to the\n   170\tworld** (§2.1):\n   171\t\n   172\t+----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   173\t| #  | Property                       | Meaning and the doctrine that names it                                                                     |\n   174\t+====+================================+============================================================================================================+\n   175\t| 1  | Observation of the world       | The result depends on world state: a file, a socket, another process, the environment, a resource's        |\n   176\t|    |                                | current condition. `abi.md` §5: \"world facts that shipped code cannot compute\".                            |\n   177\t+----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   178\t| 2  | Effect on the world            | Calling it changes world state: bytes on disk, a packet sent, a lock taken, text printed.                  |\n   179\t|    |                                | `datom.world.md:46`: \"Side effects must appear as stream emissions.\"                                       |\n   180\t+----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   181\t| 3  | World nondeterminism           | Two calls with equal arguments and equal owned state may answer differently because of the world: entropy, |\n   182\t|    |                                | a clock, an OS-assigned pid, a process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own.             |\n   183\t+----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   184\t| 4  | Asynchrony                     | The answer is not available at return, or completion invokes something later: a timer, a thread, a         |\n   185\t|    |                                | promise. `dao.stream.md`: \"No operation waits\"; `abi.md` §6: a callback is an event on a stream.           |\n   186\t+----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   187\t| 5  | World-dependent failure        | Whether it completes depends on the world (disk full, peer gone), not on the input. `dao.stream.md` Result |\n   188\t|    |                                | Convention: such outcomes are data.                                                                        |\n   189\t+----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   190\t| 6  | Another process or host        | The work runs in a different process, machine or trust domain. `abi.md` §3.                                |\n   191\t+----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   192\t\n   193\tA contact cannot be an IO action when none holds. Then it is a function of\n   194\tthe machine's values and owned state, computed synchronously in the same\n   195\tinstance, whose only failures are functions of the input, answered as data\n   196\tor a refusal. Linking a library to compute it is internal linkage (`abi.md`\n   197\t§5 `:80-81`); executing it is machine-internal work (`abi.md` §4 `:60-62`).\n   198\tThe basis of the primitive profile is therefore **internality plus purity,\n   199\ttotality and synchrony over the declared domain**, with mutation admitted\n   200\tonly as §2.4 declares it.\n   201\t\n   202\tA read of owned runtime state that may differ between calls (a weak\n   203\treference after a collection, an atom another owned thread advanced) fails\n   204\tnone of the six properties: it is a fact about the machine, not the world.\n   205\tIt is a **runtime prerequisite** (table C), neither IO nor a profile entry.\n   206\tr2 classified it as both; that contradiction is withdrawn.\n   207\t\n   208\t### 2.4 Ownership and mutation laws for the profile\n   209\t\n   210\tThe profile's purity claim is exact, not blanket:\n   211\t\n   212\t- **Pure entries** (marked P in table A) are deterministic, total over their\n   213\t  declared domain, synchronous, and allocate only fresh values; equal\n   214\t  arguments give equal results on every host.\n   215\t- **Mutating entries** (marked M: `bytes/set!`, `bytes/copy!`, `arrays/aset`,\n   216\t  `arrays/acopy`, `arrays/asort`) mutate **exactly the argument named as the\n   217\t  target** and nothing else, and never retain it. Return values are per\n   218\t  entry, not uniform: `aset` returns the assigned value (Clojure's `aset`\n   219\t  semantics, V `arrays.cljc:50-57`, unchanged); `set!`, `copy!` and `acopy`\n   220\t  return nil; `asort` returns the sorted array. Overlapping `copy!` and\n   221\t  `acopy` behave as memmove (V: `acopy` already handles same-array overlap,\n   222\t  `:97-105`). **Prevalidation**: every index and length is checked before\n   223\t  any write, so a refused call leaves the target byte-identical. This is a\n   224\t  **behaviour change** on JS and Dart, where today's `acopy` loop writes\n   225\t  element by element and can write before meeting an invalid index\n   226\t  (`arrays.cljc:97-105`, V; the JVM's `System/arraycopy` checks first); it\n   227\t  is assigned to H2 with a no-partial-write test (table D).\n   228\t- **Aliasing**: `bytes/slice`, `arrays/asub`, `arrays/aclone`, `from-vector`\n   229\t  always return a fresh value; no entry returns a view onto an argument.\n   230\t  Nothing else is promised about copying: a value appended to a stream is\n   231\t  **not** copied by the stream in general. The ring buffer stores the value\n   232\t  object itself and reads return it (`ringbuffer.cljc:124-131`, V), and the\n   233\t  ABI permits in-instance streams with no crossing and no serialization\n   234\t  (`dao.stream.wasm.md:22-27`). Aliasing across a stream is therefore\n   235\t  possible, and the rule below is what makes it safe.\n   236\t- **Exclusive mutation right, ownership transfer, and freezing\n   237\t  publication.** A mutable profile value (bytes, a host array) has exactly\n   238\t  one party with the right to mutate it. Two distinct events move or end\n   239\t  that right:\n   240\t  - **Ownership transfer** (the right moves, the value stays mutable): when\n   241\t    a profile entry marked \"fresh\" (`alloc`, `slice`, `from-vector`,\n   242\t    `magnitude-bytes`, `anew`, `aclone`, `asub`, `aconcat`, `ainsert-at`,\n   243\t    `aremove-at`, `from-coll`, `utf8-encode`) returns a value, the **caller**\n   244\t    holds the exclusive mutation right from that return, and may `set!`,\n   245\t    `copy!`, `aset`, `acopy` or `asort` it. Returning fresh storage is not\n   246\t    publication.\n   247\t  - **Freezing publication** (the right ends for everyone): appending the\n   248\t    value to a stream (in-memory or not); placing it inside a value that is\n   249\t    appended; handing it to a seam or a content handle. On publication the\n   250\t    value is **frozen**: no party, the publisher included, may mutate it\n   251\t    afterwards, and a reader may not mutate what it reads. Freezing is\n   252\t    transitive: a collection is frozen with every mutable value nested in\n   253\t    it. A party that needs a mutable copy of a frozen value takes `slice`,\n   254\t    `aclone` or `from-vector`, and then holds the right to that copy.\n   255\t- **Obligations enforceable within H0-H4**: (i) every P entry leaves every\n   256\t  argument byte-identical, asserted by a property test in H1 over the bytes,\n   257\t  text, float and integer namespaces; (ii) every M entry mutates only its\n   258\t  named target and only after prevalidation, asserted in H1 (bytes) and H2\n   259\t  (arrays); (iii) the Jing writer (H4) never mutates its input and returns\n   260\t  fresh bytes, asserted by re-encoding the same input twice and comparing\n   261\t  the input's bytes before and after; (iv) the callers of M entries inside\n   262\t  the adopted closure are enumerated and audited in H2 for the freeze rule.\n   263\t  **What the H0 gate can and cannot see (V, H0 report).** The gate resolves\n   264\t  a call as an M entry only when it goes through `dao.data.arrays` or\n   265\t  `dao.host.bytes` by alias; today that is `dao.data.btree` alone (33 calls,\n   266\t  owned node arrays, never appended). `dao.stream.chunks` (`absorb`,\n   267\t  `chunks.cljc:27-34`), `dao.jing.cbor` (`magnitude-bytes` and the\n   268\t  byte-string assembly at `:254,1313-1317`) and `yin.vm.linker.sign` (`:82`)\n   269\t  mutate host arrays **directly inside reader arms** (`System/arraycopy`,\n   270\t  `.setRange`, `.set`, `aset`) and call no M entry until H2 migrates them,\n   271\t  so the gate cannot count them now. Obligations (i)-(iii) are tests, not\n   272\t  gate checks; the freeze rule itself (no mutation after publication) is\n   273\t  not mechanically checkable by the gate at all and remains a review\n   274\t  obligation. **What H2's audit must do instead**: (a) list every direct\n   275\t  host-array mutation inside an arm across the closure (the gate's arm\n   276\t  scan plus a search for `arraycopy`, `setRange`, `.set`, `aset`, `aclone`\n   277\t  on byte arrays); (b) replace each with an M entry or a fresh-allocating P\n   278\t  entry, so that after H2 the only mutation sites in the closure are M-entry\n   279\t  calls; (c) add the migrated callers to list 2 as `:m-entry` callers with\n   280\t  their ceilings and a one-line reason each; (d) record any site that keeps\n   281\t  a direct mutation (expected: none) as a named exception. After H2 a new\n   282\t  M-entry caller fails the gate's list 2 until reviewed; before H2 the gate\n   283\t  catches only new `dao.data.arrays` callers.\n   284\t  What is **not** enforceable here: a reader mutating a frozen value it\n   285\t  obtained from a stream; that is a composition obligation stated by this\n   286\t  rule and left to review until the deferred conformance suite (§9.4).\n   287\t\n   288\t### 2.5 Borderline cases, decided\n   289\t\n   290\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   291\t| Case                              | Reasoning under §2.3                                                                                    | Verdict    |\n   292\t+===================================+=========================================================================================================+============+\n   293\t| UTF-8 decode, CBOR decode, base64 | Fallible, but the failure is a function of the input (property 5 does not hold); deterministic,         | Direct     |\n   294\t| decode, NFC normalization         | synchronous. Malformed input is a refusal value. NFC depends on a Unicode version, which the profile    | call       |\n   295\t|                                   | pins (16.0, V `debruijn.cljc:314`).                                                                     |            |\n   296\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   297\t| Hashing, signing, seed replay in  | Deterministic functions of bytes (and of the seed). The library is linkage. They execute in the same    | Direct     |\n   298\t| a library (BLAKE3, SHA-256,       | instance; for wasm, compiled in, never imported (§7). `sign.cljc:75-87`'s `SecureRandom` proxy replays  | call, in-  |\n   299\t| Ed25519; `sign.cljc:75-87`)       | the given seed bytes and is deterministic (V, R3 correction).                                           | instance   |\n   300\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   301\t| Fresh entropy, identities, key    | Property 3. Deferred design (§9); the profile contains no generator.                                    | Stream;    |\n   302\t| generation (`sign.cljc:114-124`)  |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n   303\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   304\t| Time; scheduling; the JVM         | Properties 1, 3, 4. `coordinate.cljc:80` selects `driver.clj:169-206`, which reads the clock and sleeps | Stream /   |\n   305\t| blocking content driver           | (V): JVM-lane IO inside a portable composition. Deferred (§9).                                          | driver;    |\n   306\t|                                   |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n   307\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   308\t| The Python parser                 | In-process, synchronous, deterministic over source text and the pinned grammar: internal, direct call.  | Either;    |\n   309\t|                                   | As another process: property 6, a stream. The stage shape (`parser.cljc:65-75`) serves both. Deferred.  | deferred   |\n   310\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   311\t| Same-host file read, lock, fsync, | Properties 1, 2, 5. External though same-host. Deferred (§9).                                           | Stream;    |\n   312\t| rename, directory listing         |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n   313\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   314\t| Weak and soft references,         | Owned runtime state (§2.1). Not IO, not a profile entry: table C. The B-tree's default reference mode   | Runtime    |\n   315\t| atomics, `locking`, atoms         | is `:soft` on the JVM and `:strong` on JS and Dart (V `btree.cljc:39-44`); correctness is independent   | prereq.    |\n   316\t|                                   | of the mode, only retention differs.                                                                    |            |\n   317\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   318\t| Host `=`, `hash`, `compare`,      | Internal and pure per host, not identical across hosts for mixed types and floats. A portability hazard | Dialect    |\n   319\t| `sort`, `str` of a double, regex  | only where persisted bytes depend on it; those sites sort by canonical bytes (`cbor.cljc:1070,1177`,    | semantics  |\n   320\t|                                   | `ucf/rows.cljc:139`, `debruijn.cljc:921,929`). Regex sites (`custody.cljc:53`, `datagram.cljc:86-100`,  |            |\n   321\t|                                   | `jing.cljc:107-111,216-221`, `fs.cljc:105`) match ASCII grammars identical on every host.               |            |\n   322\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   323\t| `gensym` in a compiler            | A process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own: property 3 in a weak form. Deferred   | Deferred   |\n   324\t|                                   | (§9), with the hygiene requirement the r2 review added.                                                 |            |\n   325\t+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n   326\t\n   327\t### 2.6 Where the owner's original sentence is and is not met\n   328\t\n   329\tMet for every external contact: after this design and the deferred one, a\n   330\thost's world contact is the stream seams and its `main`. Not met literally:\n   331\ta port also implements the primitive profile and meets table C, because the\n   332\tdialect's own carriers cannot be supplied by a stream and must not be. The\n   333\tprofile is exact, enumerated and oracle-checked, so that part is a list, not\n   334\ta design.\n   335\t\n   336\t## 3. Classification over the adopted closure\n   337\t\n   338\t### 3.1 The transitive closure, per lane (R, computed)\n   339\t\n   340\tMethod: a require-form traversal from `yin.repl.main`, choosing each lane's\n   341\treader arms (`:cljd` also reads `:clj`) and resolving each namespace to its\n   342\tfile under `src/<lane>` first (shadow resolution: `yin.repl.host` is\n   343\t`host.cljc` on the JVM and the `host.cljs`/`host.cljd` shadows elsewhere),\n   344\tthen `src/cljc`. It is not a compiler-resolved call graph: it can\n   345\tover-include a namespace required but unused on a path, cannot see dynamic\n   346\tloading, and **can under-include** when its reader-arm parsing fails; the\n   347\tr3 script did exactly that (it dropped requires inside `#?@` arms and\n   348\treported 141/140/139). The r3 claim that it \"cannot under-include\" is\n   349\twithdrawn. The reconciled totals, counted by **file** and confirmed by the\n   350\tr3 reviewer's independent traversal (R):\n   351\t\n   352\t+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   353\t| Lane  | Total | Shared   | Lane-specific files (V, the files exist)                                                                            |\n   354\t+=======+=======+==========+=====================================================================================================================+\n   355\t| clj   | 142   | 133      | `clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj` (blocking driver: clock, sleep, `locking`), `clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj`,    |\n   356\t|       |       |          | `clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`, `clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj` (clock, `CompletableFuture`), `clj/yang/antlr/     |\n   357\t|       |       |          | cst.clj`, `clj/yin/repl/host/jvm.clj`, `cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc` (the JVM selector),                                |\n   358\t|       |       |          | `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Nine.                                         |\n   359\t+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   360\t| cljs  | 140   | 133      | `cljs/dao/stream/datagram/node.cljs`, `cljs/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljs` (`setTimeout`), `cljs/dao/stream/ws/    |\n   361\t|       |       |          | node.cljs` (`js/Date`), `cljs/yang/antlr/cst.cljs`, `cljs/yin/repl/host.cljs` (shadow), `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/        |\n   362\t|       |       |          | boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Seven.                                                            |\n   363\t+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   364\t| cljd  | 141   | 133      | `cljd/dao/stream/cbor/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/transit/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/datagram/dart.cljd`,        |\n   365\t|       |       |          | `cljd/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljd` (`Timer`), `cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd` (`DateTime.now`), `cljd/yang/antlr/ |\n   366\t|       |       |          | cst.cljd`, `cljd/yin/repl/host.cljd` (shadow), `cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`. Eight.                               |\n   367\t+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   368\t\n   369\tThe 133 shared files are `.cljc`. H0 commits the reproducible evidence\n   370\t(§8): the traversal is re-implemented over `clojure.tools.namespace`'s\n   371\t`ns` parsing (which handles reader conditionals per platform) rather than a\n   372\tregex, committed as `src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj` (H0 placed it\n   373\tunder `src/dev/` with the repo's other dev tools), and its output as\n   374\t`docs/design/host-boundary/closure-{clj,cljs,cljd}.edn`; H0's acceptance is\n   375\tthat the committed output lists exactly the 142/140/141 files above, and\n   376\tthat the two traversals (the committed tool and the r3 reviewer's method)\n   377\tagree, recorded in the slice report. Until that lands, the totals here are\n   378\tR.\n   379\t\n   380\tClassification of every file in the closure that has any host contact (the\n   381\tremaining files have none beyond §3.3 syntax; the committed H0 output marks\n   382\teach file with its class, which is what makes \"remaining\" checkable):\n   383\t\n   384\t+-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\n   385\t| Namespaces                                          | Contact (V)                            | Class and slice      