858\t|                            | under §8.3; they are simply not list-1 entries.)                                                |                             |\n   859\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   860\t| Dialect arms (H0 finding,  | `dao/space/query.cljc` (`:808`), `yang/clojure.cljc` (`:375`), `yin/vm/engine.cljc` (`:929`): a | Stay.                       |\n   861\t| retained)                  | runtime type name in diagnostic text; `yin/repl/adapter.cljc` (`:58`), `yin/repl/dht.cljc`      |                             |\n   862\t|                            | (`:104`): the dialect's EDN reader under two namespaces; `dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc` (`:168`): |                             |\n   863\t|                            | the in-memory stream implementation's own host atom test (an implementation-private arm, §4.1). |                             |\n   864\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   865\t| Seams and driver           | `src/clj`, `src/cljs`, `src/cljd` seam and shadow files (§3.1 lane-specific list) **except**    | Stay, except the Dart Jing  |\n   866\t|                            | `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`, which is a Jing writer H4 deletes, not a seam; it is on the | writer, deleted in H4.      |\n   867\t|                            | baseline under the first row's `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc` twin. `yin/repl/main.cljc`,          |                             |\n   868\t|                            | `yin/repl/host.cljc`, `yin/repl/state.cljc`.                                                    |                             |\n   869\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   870\t| Deferred-scope files (§9)  | `dao/jing/file.cljc`, `dao/space/store/fs.cljc`, `dao/space/store.cljc`,                        | Stay until the deferred     |\n   871\t|                            | `dao/stream/journal/file.cljc`, `dao/jing/coordinate.cljc`, `dao/space/dht.cljc`,               | design lands.               |\n   872\t|                            | `dao/stream/rpc.cljc` (`:114`, the `rand` arm), `yin/repl.cljc`,                                |                             |\n   873\t|                            | `yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc` and the other `yang/python/antlr/*.cljc` with arms,             |                             |\n   874\t|                            | `dao/stream/journal.cljc` (`locking`), `dao/space/transactor.cljc`, `yin/vm/ucf/authority.cljc` |                             |\n   875\t|                            | (`locking`). (H0: `dao/jing/content/ async.cljc`, `dao/stream/udp.cljc` and                     |                             |\n   876\t|                            | `dao/space/schema.cljc` are not in the closure and are dropped from list 1; the deferred design |                             |\n   877\t|                            | records them, §9.) Every remaining closure file whose arms are only `catch`/hint/protocol       |                             |\n   878\t|                            | syntax is **not** on list 1 (exempt by pattern).                                                |                             |\n   879\t+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n   880\t\n   881\t**Baseline versus additions and deletions.** The H0 baseline is the set of\n   882\t**existing** files above (every entry except the planned additions named\n   883\tin the profile row). The planned additions are the seven `dao/host/*.cljc`\n   884\tfiles (five in H1, two in H3), which join list 1 when their slices land. The deletions are\n   885\tthree: `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc` (H2), `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc` (H4, the\n   886\tJVM/JS Boring wrapper) and `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd` (H4, the Dart\n   887\tJing writer, a lane-specific file that the seams row excludes and the first\n   888\trow lists, so that the lane-specific category cannot silently retain it).\n   889\tThe final list 1 after H4 is therefore: the baseline minus the first row\n   890\t(except `sign.cljc` for `generate`), both deleted Jing writers included in\n   891\tthat subtraction, plus the seven profile files. The host-library gate of\n   892\t§8.2 is consistent with it.\n   893\t\n   894\t**Reconciliation with H0's measurement (r7, V).** H0 generated list 1 as\n   895\t76 files: 60 matching the rows above after the corrections now in them,\n   896\tplus 16 tagged `:h0-unlisted`. The 16 are now categorised: nine in the\n   897\tfirst row (profile consumers, H2), six in the \"dialect arms\" row\n   898\t(retained), one (`rpc.cljc`) in the deferred row. **What H0 must change**:\n   899\tretag those 16 entries in the gate's list 1 and in the committed closure\n   900\tclass tags from `:h0-unlisted` to the row above (`:migration-source`,\n   901\t`:dialect-arm`, `:deferred-scope`), a data change to the test and the edn\n   902\toutputs, no tool change; drop nothing (the six names H0 could not find were\n   903\tnever on the generated list, and the rows above no longer name them).\n   904\t**What H0 need not change**: the totals 142/140/141 and 133 shared stand;\n   905\tthe traversal, its independent agreement, the two negative tests, the\n   906\tscan-failure test and the corpus hash set are unaffected; the lanes need\n   907\tnot be re-run beyond `dao.host.gate-test` itself. **How H1 and H2 clear\n   908\tthe tag**: H1 adds no consumer, so the nine stay tagged `:migration-source`;\n   909\tH2 migrates them with the other consumers and removes them from list 1 in\n   910\tthe same commit, which the `list-1-equals-the-observed-arms` test enforces.\n   911\tThe tool lives at `src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj` (H0's justified\n   912\tdeviation: the repo keeps dev tools under `src/dev/`), and the design's\n   913\tearlier `dev/host_boundary/` path is read as that.\n   914\t\n   915\t### 8.2 The host-library gate (H2, H3): executable references within the closure\n   916\t\n   917\tA literal `grep` over `src/cljc` is **not** the gate: it matches a docstring\n   918\tin the untouched `dao/data.cljc:54` (\"a BigInt/BigDecimal\", V) and the\n   919\tout-of-scope `dao/postgraphics/lowering.cljc:587` (`:rgba8unorm`, V), so\n   920\tits asserted result would be false. The gate is instead `dao.host.gate-test`\n   921\trun in **library mode**, which **lands with H2**, not H0: H0 is a freeze\n   922\twith no source change and the library mode has no consumer before H2's\n   923\tdeletion, so building it in H0 would add a deliverable nothing exercises\n   924\tand nothing can falsify; H2 builds it with the H2 name list and its two\n   925\tnegative tests (below), and H3 extends the name list. In library mode the\n   926\tgate takes the H0 closure tool's tagged file list\n   927\tfor the adopted closure (so out-of-scope files never count), reads each\n   928\tfile with the Clojure reader per lane feature (so comments and string\n   929\tliterals, docstrings included, never count), and collects symbols, class\n   930\tnames and import/require forms; a hit is any such symbol naming\n   931\t`BigInteger`, `BigDecimal`, `clojure.lang.Ratio`, `java.text`, `unorm`\n   932\t(H2) or `MessageDigest`, `Blake3`, `noble`, `blake3_dart`, `package:crypto`,\n   933\t`ed25519_edwards`, `goog.crypt.base64` (H3) in a file outside `dao/host/`\n   934\tand, for H3's Ed25519 names, outside `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (`generate`,\n   935\tdeferred). Codec-track files stay on list 1 and are not scanned by this\n   936\tmode (their arms are the codec track's). The gate fails distinctly when the\n   937\tclosure list is empty or a file cannot be read, which is a scan error, not\n   938\t\"no hits\".\n   939\t\n   940\t### 8.3 File-disjointness from the codec track, including tests (re-checked, V)\n   941\t\n   942\tCodec-track source files (`codec.md` §7.1): `dao/stream/ws.cljc`,\n   943\t`transit.cljc`, `cbor.cljc`, `cbor/boring.cljc`, `codec.cljc`,\n   944\t`remote_channel.cljc`, `jing/stream.cljc`, `data.cljc`, the four ws adapters,\n   945\t`yin/repl/{serve,connect}.cljc`. Codec-track test namespaces (`codec.md`\n   946\t§7.2): the 22 requiring Transit, including `ws_test`, `ws-codec-test`,\n   947\t`remote-route-test`, `loopback-net`, `transit-test`, `dao.stream.cbor-test`,\n   948\t`dao.jing.stream-test`, `yin.repl.main-test`.\n   949\t\n   950\tH0-H4 source files: `dao/host/*` (new), `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc`,\n   951\t`dao/jing/cbor.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`, `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`,\n   952\t`dao/jing.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`,\n   953\t`yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc`, `yin/vm/data.cljc`, `yin/vm/values.cljc`,\n   954\t`yin/vm.cljc`, `yin/vm/engine.cljc`, `yin/vm/integer.cljc`,\n   955\t`dao/stream/chunks.cljc`, `dao/stream/base64.cljc`, `dao/jing/dht.cljc`,\n   956\t`yin/vm/linker.cljc`, `dao/stream/datagram.cljc`, `dao/data/arrays.cljc`;\n   957\tand, from H0's finding (H2): `dao/space/index.cljc`, `yin/repl/query.cljc`,\n   958\t`yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc`, `yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`, `yang/php.cljc`,\n   959\t`yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc`,\n   960\t`yang/antlr/packet.cljc` (none is a codec-track file, V).\n   961\tH0-H4 test files: `dao/host/*_test` (new), `yin/vm/integer_test`,\n   962\t`yang/python/antlr/{int_heap,int_literal,int_ops}_test`, `dao/jing/cbor_*`,\n   963\t`dao/jing_test`, `yin/vm/linker/sign_test`, `dao/stream/base64_test`,\n   964\t`yin/vm/debruijn_code_test`, `yin/vm/data_test`, `dao/data/btree_*`.\n   965\t\n   966\tIntersection: none, with two recorded deferrals: `dao/stream/codec.cljc:45`\n   967\t(`host-bytes?` over `dao.host.bytes`) after C4, and `dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`\n   968\t(the transport writer) is **not** touched by H4. The `dao.stream.cbor-test`\n   969\tand `dao.jing.cbor_test` namespaces are distinct files. The deferred H5\n   970\t(§9.1) would touch `ws.cljc:122`, `ws_test.cljc:11` and\n   971\t`remote_route_test.cljc:24` (V: identity-less ring creation), which is why\n   972\tit is deferred behind the codec track as well as behind its own design.\n   973\t\n   974\tCoordination with a host-lane probe track: its \"narrow source adaptations\"\n   975\t(R: the spike touched `integer/host.cljc`, `jing.cljc`, `cbor.cljc`,\n   976\t`cbor/boring.cljc`) are exactly the H1-H4 files; under OQ3 that work is\n   977\tpaused until H4 lands and needs file-level coordination, not just a rebase.\n   978\t\n   979\t## 9. DEFERRED scope (not commissionable under this document)\n   980\t\n   981\tThe following keep their design intent from r2 but are **not commissionable\n   982\there**. They belong to a separate design, proposed name\n   983\t`docs/design/dao.stream.host-effects.md`, with its own non-Claude review.\n   984\tNothing below is decided; the \"required inputs\" are the findings a\n   985\tsuccessor must answer before slices exist. Slice ids H5-H8 are reserved so\n   986\tno other document's citation moves.\n   987\t\n   988\t### 9.1 Entropy, identities, scheduling, ambient facts, printing (reserved H5)\n   989\t\n   990\tIntent: entropy as data the driver deposits (events in); identities derived\n   991\tfrom drawn bytes and carried in creation specs; the driver owns pumping and\n   992\twake-up; ambient facts and the one core `println` as data; `gensym` with\n   993\thygienic naming. Required inputs (from the r2 review R2, R6 and §3.2):\n   994\t\n   995\t- exclusive allocation and resumable consumption of an entropy stream, given\n   996\t  that cursors are non-destructive (`dao/stream.cljc:159-173`) and\n   997\t  independent readers could reuse bytes; partial batches and loss; blocked\n   998\t  resumption;\n   999\t- bootstrap identities: the entropy stream itself needs an identity before\n  1000\t  any byte is drawn;\n  1001\t- preserved UUID spelling: `ucf/custody.cljc:50-56` requires the\n  1002\t  8-4-4-4-12 lowercase form, so a derived id must keep that grammar;\n  1003\t- DHT initialization draws several values synchronously\n  1004\t  (`space/dht.cljc:255-269`, R): failure and continuation behaviour;\n  1005\t- async completions emitted as data: moving consumer callbacks into a\n  1006\t  `step!` does not remove them (R2); the `btree.storage` promise facade and\n  1007\t  `content.async` consumers need a data-shaped completion;\n  1008\t- the JVM blocking content driver selected at `coordinate.cljc:80`\n  1009\t  (`driver.clj:169-206`: clock and sleep) is lane-specific IO inside a\n  1010\t  portable composition and needs its own disposition;\n  1011\t- the codec-track test edits `ws_test.cljc:11` and `remote_route_test.cljc:24`\n  1012\t  and the `ws.cljc:122` site: staged compatibility or an explicit dependency\n  1013\t  on C4; a re-estimate of the slice (the r2 estimate of about twenty files\n  1014\t  was understated: UCF custody and holder migrations are included);\n  1015\t- `gensym` hygiene: compiler counters need hygienic naming, not a reset;\n  1016\t  `transact.cljc:35` tempids need separate treatment;\n  1017\t- OQ9 (entropy shape: stream versus identity-in-spec with driver-minted\n  1018\t  secrets; both avoid core generators, the r2 recommendation overclaimed)\n  1019\t  and OQ10 (compiler `gensym`) move to that design;\n  1020\t- the five `random-uuid` sites outside the transitive closure\n  1021\t  (`dao/await.cljc:235,240`, `yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve.cljc:417,604,606`,\n  1022\t  V, H0) and the out-of-closure `dao/jing/content/async.cljc` timer and\n  1023\t  `dao/stream/udp.cljc` arms: the deferred design decides whether its scope\n  1024\t  is the transitive closure (then they are out until required) or the\n  1025\t  directory scope of OQ1 (then they are in).\n  1026\t\n  1027\t### 9.2 The durable-storage seam (reserved H6)\n  1028\t\n  1029\tIntent: one seam beneath the journal with enumerated lower operations; the\n  1030\tclaim-file algorithm portable; capability map (`lock-kind`,\n  1031\t`directory-sync?`, `failure-model`). Required inputs (R5, A7): the atomic\n  1032\treplacement at `store/fs.cljc:352-387` renames **before** the directory\n  1033\tsync (`:364-370`, `:382-383`, V), so a sync failure can leave the new HEAD\n  1034\tvisible; the seam must distinguish pre-rename failure (target unchanged),\n  1035\tpost-rename visibility (new HEAD visible, durability uncertain) and sync\n  1036\tuncertainty, and test post-rename failure explicitly; short reads, partial\n  1037\tappends, acquisition outcomes (`held`/`refused`/`unsupported`), durability\n  1038\toutcomes per the declared failure model; `own-pid`, `process-alive?`,\n  1039\t`create-exclusive!`, `unlink!`, `canonical-path` for the claim-file lock.\n  1040\t\n  1041\t### 9.3 The parser stage (reserved H7)\n  1042\t\n  1043\tIntent: the grammar profile plus host walk as one seam value; a\n  1044\tremote-stage test. Required input: none beyond the stage's own host walk;\n  1045\tr3's \"one `println`\" at `yang/python/antlr/repl.cljc:71` was inside a\n  1046\tquoted yin program (the VM's primitive, not a host call, V) and is\n  1047\twithdrawn.\n  1048\t\n  1049\t### 9.4 Stream-implementation conformance and the wasm import gate (reserved H8)\n  1050\t\n  1051\tIntent: the contract's outcome tables through a scripted seam against every\n  1052\timplementation; a gate asserting a wasm binary's import list against the\n  1053\tseven names. Required input: which implementations declare which outcome\n  1054\tsubset (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n  1055\t\n  1056\t## 10. Cost and what would reverse this (H0-H4)\n  1057\t\n  1058\tCost (P, from §1 and §8.3, revised r7 for H0's finding): **24 existing\n  1059\tfiles edited** (the 16 of r5 plus the eight profile consumers H0 found:\n  1060\t`dao/space/index.cljc`, `yin/repl/query.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc`,\n  1061\t`yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`, `yang/php.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc`,\n  1062\t`yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc`, `yang/antlr/packet.cljc`; each a\n  1063\trequire plus one arm, so `dao.space.index` and the UCF rows file are no\n  1064\tlonger \"untouched\") and **3 deleted**\n  1065\t(`yin/vm/integer/host.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`,\n  1066\t`src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`), **7** `dao.host.*` files added (five in\n  1067\tH1, two in H3); **at least 5** test namespaces added (`gate-test` in H0,\n  1068\t`conformance-test`, `bytes-test`, `text-test` in H1, `hash-test` in H3),\n  1069\tthe 3 Python integer tests and `integer_test` re-pointed; about 10^3 tests\n  1070\trerun per lane (R: JVM quick tier about 4,000); about 10^1 rewritten.\n  1071\tBase64 and the Jing writer are replacements, not code motion, and are\n  1072\taccepted only by their byte oracles. No performance figure is claimed; H4\n  1073\tmeasures.\n  1074\t\n  1075\tRisk, revised (review N4): the laws of §2.4 and table A are **not verbatim\n  1076\textraction** in two places, and each is a behaviour change with a direct\n  1077\ttest: `acopy` prevalidation on JS and Dart (H2) and strict `utf8-encode`\n  1078\trefusal where today's helpers substitute (H1 law, H3 consumers). The `aset`\n  1079\treturn value is kept as Clojure's: unchanged, stated only so the law cannot\n  1080\tbe read as changing it. H1 medium: new namespaces plus two new laws and\n  1081\ttheir property tests.\n  1082\tH2 medium: seventeen consumers (nine of r5 plus the eight H0 found, two of\n  1083\tthem with named behaviour changes, §3.1), the Jing carrier move, the arrays\n  1084\tprevalidation change and the M-caller audit in one slice; mitigated by the corpus, the\n  1085\tpre-deletion closure-tool gate and splitting the slice per namespace if the report\n  1086\tshows drift. H3 low-to-medium: the UTF-8 behaviour change is observable only\n  1087\ton unpaired-surrogate strings, which the codec already refuses. H4\n  1088\tlow-to-medium: the corpus decides; performance unknown until measured. The\n  1089\tClojureDart traps are named in §14.\n  1090\t\n  1091\tReversal: an owner ruling that reader arms are the intended port mechanism;\n  1092\ta measured H4 regression that matters (the portable writer stays as oracle\n  1093\tand a host supplies a faster one behind the same profile); owner rejection\n  1094\tof §12 (then §2 stands as this document's reading of the existing ABI text).\n  1095\t\n  1096\t## 11. Decision record\n  1097\t\n  1098\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1099\t| Id   | Decision                                                                                                         | Status                       |\n  1100\t+======+==================================================================================================================+==============================+\n  1101\t| HD1  | The governing test: dao.stream wherever the contact can be an IO action with respect to the world (§2.1-2.3); an | Owner rule (\"test 2          |\n  1102\t|      | internal pure, total, synchronous function, or a declared mutation of an owned argument, may be a direct call.   | governs\"); r3 wording needs  |\n  1103\t|      |                                                                                                                  | non-Claude sign-off          |\n  1104\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1105\t| HD2  | Internal contacts form the primitive profile: 76 entries (§4.2 table A) with kinds P/M and the §2.4 ownership    | Architect r3; needs sign-off |\n  1106\t|      | laws, plus runtime prerequisites (table C); in-instance for wasm (§7).                                           |                              |\n  1107\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1108\t| HD3  | Commissionable scope is §2 plus H0-H4; entropy, scheduling, storage, parser stage and stream conformance are     | Owner, 2026-10-20 (\"yes,     |\n  1109\t|      | deferred to a separate design with the §9 required inputs.                                                       | proceed with the split\")     |\n  1110\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1111\t| HD4  | H0 gates both reader arms and resolved effect calls over the committed per-lane closure; list 1 names retained   | Architect r3                 |\n  1112\t|      | arms; H2's deletion is gated by the closure tool reading `ns` forms: the shim required by no file before, absent |                              |\n  1113\t|      | and required by no file after.                                                                                   |                              |\n  1114\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1115\t| HD5  | H0-H4 are file-disjoint from the codec track in source and tests, with one deferred one-line edit                | Architect r3                 |\n  1116\t|      | (`codec.cljc:45`); host-lane source adaptations in H1-H4 files are paused (OQ3) and need file-level              |                              |\n  1117\t|      | coordination.                                                                                                    |                              |\n  1118\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1119\t\n  1120\t### 11.1 Owner decisions recorded (2026-10-20)\n  1121\t\n  1122\tThe owner adopted all six r1 recommendations: \"agree with all of fable's\n  1123\trecommendations\"; for OQ5 separately: \"makes sense. i agree with fable's\n  1124\trecommendation\"; for the r3 split: \"yes, proceed with the split\".\n  1125\t\n  1126\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1127\t| Id   | Decision as adopted                                                                                              | Status                       |\n  1128\t+======+==================================================================================================================+==============================+\n  1129\t| OQ1  | Scope is the `yin.repl` closure; GUI, graphics and demo namespaces excluded.                                     | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n  1130\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1131\t| OQ2  | The profile family is `dao.host.*`; `dao.data.arrays` stays in place as a profile member by reference.           | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n  1132\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1133\t| OQ3  | Arm-adding work in the files H1-H3 touch is paused until phase A lands; boundary-agnostic probes proceed.        | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n  1134\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1135\t| OQ4  | Boring and the Dart `cbor` package are dropped from the Jing content path only; the transport codec keeps them.  | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n  1136\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1137\t| OQ5  | Storage is a seam beneath the journal, not a streams-all-the-way-down redesign (now deferred, §9.2).             | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n  1138\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1139\t| OQ6  | This track has its own orchestrator seat, independent of the codec track and any host-lane track.                | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n  1140\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1141\t| Spli | Commissionable scope is the rule plus H0-H4; the rest is deferred to a separate design.                          | Owner, 2026-10-20            |\n  1142\t| t    |                                                                                                                  |                              |\n  1143\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n  1144\t\n  1145\t## 12. OWNER PROPOSAL: amendment to datom.world.abi.md (not doctrine; not applied)\n  1146\t\n  1147\tThis section is a proposal for the owner's approval (OQ8). It is **not**\n  1148\tverbatim doctrine and nothing in `datom.world.abi.md` or `datom.world.md`\n  1149\thas been edited. The owner's quoted words are marked as such; the rest is\n  1150\tthe Architect's proposed wording, to be revised by the owner.\n  1151\t\n  1152\t**12.1 Proposed new §8 of `datom.world.abi.md`, \"The governing test\".**\n  1153\t\n  1154\t> Owner ruling, 2026-10-20 (verbatim): \"when the host boundary can be an IO\n  1155\t> monad then dao.stream is used. If it cannot be a monad than dao.stream\n  1156\t> doesnt have to be used\"; and, asked whether same-host-ness or the\n  1157\t> IO-action test governs: \"yes, test 2 governs\".\n  1158\t>\n  1159\t> Proposed wording. The machine is the running instance and the runtime\n  1160\t> state it owns; the world is everything else. A host contact uses\n  1161\t> `dao.stream` when it can be an IO action with respect to the world: its\n  1162\t> result depends on world state; it changes world state; equal calls may\n  1163\t> answer differently because of the world; its answer is not available at\n  1164\t> return; whether it completes depends on the world; or it runs in another\n  1165\t> process, host or trust domain. A same-host effect (a file read, a clock\n  1166\t> reading, entropy) is an IO action and so a stream.\n  1167\t>\n  1168\t> A contact that is a pure, total, synchronous function of the machine's\n  1169\t> values, or a declared mutation of a value the caller owns, may be a direct\n  1170\t> call. Such contacts are enumerated in a host primitive profile with an\n  1171\t> oracle per entry ([dao.stream.host-boundary.md](./dao.stream.host-boundary.md)\n  1172\t> §4.2), execute in-instance (for wasm: compiled in, never imported), and\n  1173\t> add no contract to `dao.stream`. Reads of owned runtime state (a weak\n  1174\t> reference, an atom) are neither IO nor profile entries; they are the\n  1175\t> dialect's runtime prerequisites. Linking a library to compute a profile\n  1176\t> entry is internal linkage (§5).\n  1177\t>\n  1178\t> §7's two non-adapters are read under this test: a map of functions that\n  1179\t> portable application code calls to reach the world is a host service and\n  1180\t> is rejected; a seam a stream implementation calls privately to perform its\n  1181\t> own IO is that implementation's adapter and is not.\n  1182\t\n  1183\t**12.2 Proposed change to `datom.world.md:70`.**\n  1184\t\n  1185\tToday: \"A host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary;\n  1186\timplementations do not add contracts.\"\n  1187\t\n  1188\tProposed: \"A host boundary that can be an IO action is the dao.stream\n  1189\tabstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts. A pure, total,\n  1190\tsynchronous host function is a primitive-profile entry, not a boundary\n  1191\t([datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §8).\"\n  1192\t\n  1193\t## 13. Open owner decisions\n  1194\t\n  1195\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n  1196\t| Id   | Question                                                                                             | Architect's recommendation (not a decision)        |\n  1197\t+======+======================================================================================================+====================================================+\n  1198\t| OQ7  | H4's measurement: what regression, if any, is acceptable for the portable Jing writer against Boring | Accept any regression under corpus identity; set a |\n  1199\t|      | on the JVM and Node.                                                                                 | bound after H4's numbers exist, not before.        |\n  1200\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n  1201\t| OQ8  | Approve the §12 proposal (the ABI's new §8 and the one-sentence change to `datom.world.md:70`).      | Approve after the owner revises the proposed       |\n  1202\t|      |                                                                                                      | wording; §2 stands either way as this document's   |\n  1203\t|      |                                                                                                      | reading.                                           |\n  1204\t+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n  1205\t\n  1206\tOQ1-OQ6 are closed (§11.1). OQ9 and OQ10 belong to the deferred design\n  1207\t(§9.1).\n  1208\t\n  1209\t## 14. For a successor orchestrator\n  1210\t\n  1211\tDecided (HD1-HD5, OQ1-OQ6, the split): the rule, the exact profile with\n  1212\townership laws, the H0-H4 sequence and gates, the deferral. Open: OQ7, OQ8;\n  1213\tneither blocks H0-H4.\n  1214\t\n  1215\tStart with **H0**: it changes no source; commit the closure tool\n  1216\t(`src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj`, over `clojure.tools.namespace`), its\n  1217\tper-lane output under `docs/design/host-boundary/` tagged by class, and the\n  1218\tgate test; confirm the 142/140/141 totals against an independent traversal\n  1219\t(done, H0 report) and finish the r7 reconciliation of list 1 (§8.1: retag\n  1220\tthe 16 `:h0-unlisted` entries, a data change only) before H1; a non-Claude\n  1221\treviewer confirms r7 and the retagged H0 before H1 is dispatched. Then H1 (new files,\n  1222\ttheir direct tests, and the two new laws: strict `utf8-encode` and M-entry\n  1223\tprevalidation). H2 is the first deletion; its gate is the closure tool\n  1224\treading `ns` forms (the shim required by no file before the deletion commit,\n  1225\tabsent and required by no file after), and it carries the\n  1226\t`acopy` prevalidation change and the M-caller audit (§2.4). H3 carries the\n  1227\tnamed UTF-8 behaviour change for its two consumers. H3 and H4 in either\n  1228\torder; slow tier once after H4. Do not start anything in §9; a separate\n  1229\tdesign document and its own non-Claude sign-off come first.\n  1230\t\n  1231\tLanes and gates (`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`): iterate on the JVM lane\n  1232\twith `bb test:sub:clj dao.jing` or `yin.vm` (G0); three-lane subsystem test\n  1233\tat each checkpoint (G1); `bb test:changed` across all three lanes in the\n  1234\tworktree before landing (G2); `bb test` on master after merge (G3). Record\n  1235\tper-lane counts before and after every slice. The Dart lane needs `flutter`\n  1236\ton PATH; a fresh worktree needs `npm ci`. ClojureDart traps for exactly this\n  1237\tkind of file: `:cljd` first in every mixed conditional; a tail-position\n  1238\t`:cljd nil` silently fails; protocol method parameters never `[_ _]`;\n  1239\t`(- 0.0)` is `+0.0`; multi-key `assoc` on nil misbehaves.\n  1240\t\n  1241\tReview requirement: this design and any implementation an orchestrator\n  1242\tderives from it under a Claude model are Claude-authored. **Sign-off on this\n  1243\tfinal text (§2 plus H0-H4) and on each slice must come from a non-Claude\n  1244\tarchitect**; a Claude reviewer may review but may not be the sole sign-off.\n  1245\tThe reviewer checks, in order: that every entry in table A is internal and\n  1246\tpure or declared mutating under §2.4, with the freeze rule's enforceable\n  1247\tobligations (i)-(iv) each backed by a test or an audited list; that H0's\n  1248\tcommitted closure output matches 142/140/141 and list 1 matches the §8.1\n  1249\tbaseline by category; that the two negative tests and the scan-failure test\n  1250\tfail as intended; that H2's closure-tool gates hold before and after the deletion\n  1251\tcommit and the no-partial-write tests exist; that H1-H4 acceptance is\n  1252\tbyte-identical against the named corpus with errata preserved and the two\n  1253\tbehaviour changes are named in slice reports; that no H0-H4 edit touches a\n  1254\tcodec-track source or test file except the recorded `codec.cljc:45` deferral;\n  1255\tthat nothing from §9 is started.\n  1256\t\n  1257\t## Appendix A. Citations opened for this document (2026-10-20, read only)\n  1258\t\n  1259\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1260\t| Cite                                                         | Finding                                                                                          |\n  1261\t+==============================================================+==================================================================================================+\n  1262\t| `docs/design/datom.world.md:16-32,43-48,68-71`               | Axioms; side effects as emissions; the sentence §12.2 proposes to change.                        |\n  1263\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1264\t| `docs/design/datom.world.abi.md:21-24,35-44,47-52,56-64,`    | One ABI; custody; machine-internal work in-instance; the floor and internal linkage; effects     |\n  1265\t| `75-87,94-148`                                               | out, events in; adapter discipline; the two non-adapters.                                        |\n  1266\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1267\t| `docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:29-52`                       | Seven imports; primitives have no imports.                                                       |\n  1268\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1269\t| `docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md:140-180,658-780`            | Contract namespace; slice file set; the 22 test namespaces (§8.3).                               |\n  1270\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1271\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer/host.cljc:1-298`                    | 25 public functions; \"depends on nothing\".                                                       |\n  1272\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1273\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:1-101`; `yin/vm.cljc:32,929`;  | Consumers of the shim (requires and `big-carrier?` sites).                                       |\n  1274\t| `yin/vm/engine.cljc:38,810`; `yin/vm/values.cljc:30,196`;    |                                                                                                  |\n  1275\t| `yin/vm/data.cljc:41,421,547`; `test/yang/python/antlr/`     |                                                                                                  |\n  1276\t| `{int_heap,int_literal,int_ops}_test.cljc`                   |                                                                                                  |\n  1277\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1278\t| `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1-60,95-254,342-460,483-632,`   | Reader; big-integer helpers; carriers with equality and hash; JVM Decimal/Ratio construction and |\n  1279\t| `733-784,1070,1177`                                          | access; byte-ordered sorting.                                                                    |\n  1280\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1281\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:228-245,310-317`         | Float64 little-endian hex; JVM big-integer predicate (assigned to H2).                           |\n  1282\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1283\t| `src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:36-44,87-119,182-245`;         | Reference-mode default `:soft` on the JVM; atomic flag; weak/soft references; 12 array           |\n  1284\t| `src/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:28-182`                       | functions.                                                                                       |\n  1285\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1286\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc:1-130`                     | Libraries; `jvm-pair` deterministic seed replay (`:75-87`); `generate` fresh entropy             |\n  1287\t|                                                              | (`:114-124`).                                                                                    |\n  1288\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1289\t| `src/cljc/dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:63-82`;                   | JVM selection of the blocking driver; clock and sleep in the driver.                             |\n  1290\t| `src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj:160-209`                |                                                                                                  |\n  1291\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1292\t| `src/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc:84-261,340-387`            | Claim-file lock; atomic replace renames before directory sync (§9.2 input).                      |\n  1293\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1294\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/custody.cljc:40-56`                     | UUID grammar for occurrence ids (§9.1 input).                                                    |\n  1295\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1296\t| Regex: `custody.cljc:53`, `datagram.cljc:80-130`,            | ASCII grammars; dialect semantics.                                                               |\n  1297\t| `jing.cljc:107-111,216-221`, `fs.cljc:105`                   |                                                                                                  |\n  1298\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1299\t| `test/dao/stream/ws_test.cljc:11`,                           | Identity-less ring creation in codec-track tests (§8.3, §9.1).                                   |\n  1300\t| `test/dao/stream/remote_route_test.cljc:24`                  |                                                                                                  |\n  1301\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1302\t| `src/cljc/dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:14`                 | The 27th `random-uuid`, out of scope.                                                            |\n  1303\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1304\t| Closure traversals (R), three lanes: the r3 script           | 142/140/141 files, 133 shared, by file after the r3 script's `#?@` parse defect was found; the   |\n  1305\t| (superseded) and the r3 reviewer's independent traversal     | lane-only members and the per-file markers of §3.1.                                              |\n  1306\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1307\t| `rg '^\\(deftest'` over the table D files                     | Deftest names and counts: integer 25, sign 4, base64 3, data 24, hash registry 15, cbor fixtures |\n  1308\t|                                                              | 14, cbor conformance 14, int contract 12, jing 39, cbor 21, file 17, debruijn 80, debruijn code  |\n  1309\t|                                                              | 26.                                                                                              |\n  1310\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1311\t| `test/resources/dao/jing/{cbor-v1.json,cbor-v1.errata.md,`   | Corpus and errata; digest table; sign vectors; integer fixtures; CPython int contract.           |\n  1312\t| `digest-table.edn}`; `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn`;  |                                                                                                  |\n  1313\t| `test/yin/vm/integer_v3_fixtures.cljc`;                      |                                                                                                  |\n  1314\t| `test/resources/yang/python/int-contract-v1.txt`             |                                                                                                  |\n  1315\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1316\t| `git log` (`8351e639`, `eacaed90`); `docs/orchestrator-log.` | Codec C0, C1 landed; R lane counts.                                                              |\n  1317\t| `md:11739,12375`                                             |                                                                                                  |\n  1318\t+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '230,440p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md; sed -n '740,990p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '230,440p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md; sed -n '740,990p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\"","aggregated_output":"  Nothing else is promised about copying: a value appended to a stream is\n  **not** copied by the stream in general. The ring buffer stores the value\n  object itself and reads return it (`ringbuffer.cljc:124-131`, V), and the\n  ABI permits in-instance streams with no crossing and no serialization\n  (`dao.stream.wasm.md:22-27`). Aliasing across a stream is therefore\n  possible, and the rule below is what makes it safe.\n- **Exclusive mutation right, ownership transfer, and freezing\n  publication.** A mutable profile value (bytes, a host array) has exactly\n  one party with the right to mutate it. Two distinct events move or end\n  that right:\n  - **Ownership transfer** (the right moves, the value stays mutable): when\n    a profile entry marked \"fresh\" (`alloc`, `slice`, `from-vector`,\n    `magnitude-bytes`, `anew`, `aclone`, `asub`, `aconcat`, `ainsert-at`,\n    `aremove-at`, `from-coll`, `utf8-encode`) returns a value, the **caller**\n    holds the exclusive mutation right from that return, and may `set!`,\n    `copy!`, `aset`, `acopy` or `asort` it. Returning fresh storage is not\n    publication.\n  - **Freezing publication** (the right ends for everyone): appending the\n    value to a stream (in-memory or not); placing it inside a value that is\n    appended; handing it to a seam or a content handle. On publication the\n    value is **frozen**: no party, the publisher included, may mutate it\n    afterwards, and a reader may not mutate what it reads. Freezing is\n    transitive: a collection is frozen with every mutable value nested in\n    it. A party that needs a mutable copy of a frozen value takes `slice`,\n    `aclone` or `from-vector`, and then holds the right to that copy.\n- **Obligations enforceable within H0-H4**: (i) every P entry leaves every\n  argument byte-identical, asserted by a property test in H1 over the bytes,\n  text, float and integer namespaces; (ii) every M entry mutates only its\n  named target and only after prevalidation, asserted in H1 (bytes) and H2\n  (arrays); (iii) the Jing writer (H4) never mutates its input and returns\n  fresh bytes, asserted by re-encoding the same input twice and comparing\n  the input's bytes before and after; (iv) the callers of M entries inside\n  the adopted closure are enumerated and audited in H2 for the freeze rule.\n  **What the H0 gate can and cannot see (V, H0 report).** The gate resolves\n  a call as an M entry only when it goes through `dao.data.arrays` or\n  `dao.host.bytes` by alias; today that is `dao.data.btree` alone (33 calls,\n  owned node arrays, never appended). `dao.stream.chunks` (`absorb`,\n  `chunks.cljc:27-34`), `dao.jing.cbor` (`magnitude-bytes` and the\n  byte-string assembly at `:254,1313-1317`) and `yin.vm.linker.sign` (`:82`)\n  mutate host arrays **directly inside reader arms** (`System/arraycopy`,\n  `.setRange`, `.set`, `aset`) and call no M entry until H2 migrates them,\n  so the gate cannot count them now. Obligations (i)-(iii) are tests, not\n  gate checks; the freeze rule itself (no mutation after publication) is\n  not mechanically checkable by the gate at all and remains a review\n  obligation. **What H2's audit must do instead**: (a) list every direct\n  host-array mutation inside an arm across the closure (the gate's arm\n  scan plus a search for `arraycopy`, `setRange`, `.set`, `aset`, `aclone`\n  on byte arrays); (b) replace each with an M entry or a fresh-allocating P\n  entry, so that after H2 the only mutation sites in the closure are M-entry\n  calls; (c) add the migrated callers to list 2 as `:m-entry` callers with\n  their ceilings and a one-line reason each; (d) record any site that keeps\n  a direct mutation (expected: none) as a named exception. After H2 a new\n  M-entry caller fails the gate's list 2 until reviewed; before H2 the gate\n  catches only new `dao.data.arrays` callers.\n  What is **not** enforceable here: a reader mutating a frozen value it\n  obtained from a stream; that is a composition obligation stated by this\n  rule and left to review until the deferred conformance suite (§9.4).\n\n### 2.5 Borderline cases, decided\n\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Case                              | Reasoning under §2.3                                                                                    | Verdict    |\n+===================================+=========================================================================================================+============+\n| UTF-8 decode, CBOR decode, base64 | Fallible, but the failure is a function of the input (property 5 does not hold); deterministic,         | Direct     |\n| decode, NFC normalization         | synchronous. Malformed input is a refusal value. NFC depends on a Unicode version, which the profile    | call       |\n|                                   | pins (16.0, V `debruijn.cljc:314`).                                                                     |            |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Hashing, signing, seed replay in  | Deterministic functions of bytes (and of the seed). The library is linkage. They execute in the same    | Direct     |\n| a library (BLAKE3, SHA-256,       | instance; for wasm, compiled in, never imported (§7). `sign.cljc:75-87`'s `SecureRandom` proxy replays  | call, in-  |\n| Ed25519; `sign.cljc:75-87`)       | the given seed bytes and is deterministic (V, R3 correction).                                           | instance   |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Fresh entropy, identities, key    | Property 3. Deferred design (§9); the profile contains no generator.                                    | Stream;    |\n| generation (`sign.cljc:114-124`)  |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Time; scheduling; the JVM         | Properties 1, 3, 4. `coordinate.cljc:80` selects `driver.clj:169-206`, which reads the clock and sleeps | Stream /   |\n| blocking content driver           | (V): JVM-lane IO inside a portable composition. Deferred (§9).                                          | driver;    |\n|                                   |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| The Python parser                 | In-process, synchronous, deterministic over source text and the pinned grammar: internal, direct call.  | Either;    |\n|                                   | As another process: property 6, a stream. The stage shape (`parser.cljc:65-75`) serves both. Deferred.  | deferred   |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Same-host file read, lock, fsync, | Properties 1, 2, 5. External though same-host. Deferred (§9).                                           | Stream;    |\n| rename, directory listing         |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Weak and soft references,         | Owned runtime state (§2.1). Not IO, not a profile entry: table C. The B-tree's default reference mode   | Runtime    |\n| atomics, `locking`, atoms         | is `:soft` on the JVM and `:strong` on JS and Dart (V `btree.cljc:39-44`); correctness is independent   | prereq.    |\n|                                   | of the mode, only retention differs.                                                                    |            |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| Host `=`, `hash`, `compare`,      | Internal and pure per host, not identical across hosts for mixed types and floats. A portability hazard | Dialect    |\n| `sort`, `str` of a double, regex  | only where persisted bytes depend on it; those sites sort by canonical bytes (`cbor.cljc:1070,1177`,    | semantics  |\n|                                   | `ucf/rows.cljc:139`, `debruijn.cljc:921,929`). Regex sites (`custody.cljc:53`, `datagram.cljc:86-100`,  |            |\n|                                   | `jing.cljc:107-111,216-221`, `fs.cljc:105`) match ASCII grammars identical on every host.               |            |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n| `gensym` in a compiler            | A process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own: property 3 in a weak form. Deferred   | Deferred   |\n|                                   | (§9), with the hygiene requirement the r2 review added.                                                 |            |\n+-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n\n### 2.6 Where the owner's original sentence is and is not met\n\nMet for every external contact: after this design and the deferred one, a\nhost's world contact is the stream seams and its `main`. Not met literally:\na port also implements the primitive profile and meets table C, because the\ndialect's own carriers cannot be supplied by a stream and must not be. The\nprofile is exact, enumerated and oracle-checked, so that part is a list, not\na design.\n\n## 3. Classification over the adopted closure\n\n### 3.1 The transitive closure, per lane (R, computed)\n\nMethod: a require-form traversal from `yin.repl.main`, choosing each lane's\nreader arms (`:cljd` also reads `:clj`) and resolving each namespace to its\nfile under `src/<lane>` first (shadow resolution: `yin.repl.host` is\n`host.cljc` on the JVM and the `host.cljs`/`host.cljd` shadows elsewhere),\nthen `src/cljc`. It is not a compiler-resolved call graph: it can\nover-include a namespace required but unused on a path, cannot see dynamic\nloading, and **can under-include** when its reader-arm parsing fails; the\nr3 script did exactly that (it dropped requires inside `#?@` arms and\nreported 141/140/139). The r3 claim that it \"cannot under-include\" is\nwithdrawn. The reconciled totals, counted by **file** and confirmed by the\nr3 reviewer's independent traversal (R):\n\n+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Lane  | Total | Shared   | Lane-specific files (V, the files exist)                                                                            |\n+=======+=======+==========+=====================================================================================================================+\n| clj   | 142   | 133      | `clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj` (blocking driver: clock, sleep, `locking`), `clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj`,    |\n|       |       |          | `clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`, `clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj` (clock, `CompletableFuture`), `clj/yang/antlr/     |\n|       |       |          | cst.clj`, `clj/yin/repl/host/jvm.clj`, `cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc` (the JVM selector),                                |\n|       |       |          | `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Nine.                                         |\n+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| cljs  | 140   | 133      | `cljs/dao/stream/datagram/node.cljs`, `cljs/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljs` (`setTimeout`), `cljs/dao/stream/ws/    |\n|       |       |          | node.cljs` (`js/Date`), `cljs/yang/antlr/cst.cljs`, `cljs/yin/repl/host.cljs` (shadow), `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/        |\n|       |       |          | boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Seven.                                                            |\n+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| cljd  | 141   | 133      | `cljd/dao/stream/cbor/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/transit/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/datagram/dart.cljd`,        |\n|       |       |          | `cljd/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljd` (`Timer`), `cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd` (`DateTime.now`), `cljd/yang/antlr/ |\n|       |       |          | cst.cljd`, `cljd/yin/repl/host.cljd` (shadow), `cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`. Eight.                               |\n+-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nThe 133 shared files are `.cljc`. H0 commits the reproducible evidence\n(§8): the traversal is re-implemented over `clojure.tools.namespace`'s\n`ns` parsing (which handles reader conditionals per platform) rather tha